sweet: mount the root volume with its journal, even if fsck rewrote the header

A volume created with "mkfs.hfsplus -J" / "newfs_hfs -J" keeps its journal
(journal info block + embedded journal) even when "fsck.hfsplus -f -y" rewrites
the volume header and loses the "the last mounter was journal aware" marker
(vhp->lastMountedVersion != kHFSJMountVersion).  Apple's driver then never
opens the journal: every metadata update goes straight to the disk (no
transaction, no atomicity, no replay), hfs_start_transaction() takes the
global HFS lock exclusively (the create path deadlocks against it) and a
hard power-off leaves the volume inconsistent.

This patch detects the journal physically (the journal info block at
vhp->journalInfoBlock has to be readable and has to describe an embedded
journal inside the volume) and mounts the volume with the journal enabled
anyway.  A volume without a journal is not touched at all.  A journal that
was used by a mount that was not journal aware is discarded instead of
replayed (the same thing hfs_late_journal_init() does), so a stale journal
can never be replayed over a volume that was written behind our back.

Boot arg "sweetfsjournal=0" turns the detection off again (comparison boot).

